  "response": "## Solution\n\n**Given:**\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002\n- Sensitivity: P(T+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(T−|¬D) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(T+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Apply Bayes' theorem:**\n\nP(D|T+) = [P(T+|D)·P(D)] / [P(T+|D)·P(D) + P(T+|¬D)·P(¬D)]\n\nNumerator: 0.98 × 0.002 = 0.00196\n\nDenominator: 0.00196 + (0.05 × 0.998) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\nP(D|T+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794 = **3.78%**\n\nThis is the classic base-rate/false-positive paradox: even with a fairly accurate test, because the condition is rare, most positive results are false positives.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
